(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-supraventricular-arrhythmias-vignettes"]={"title":"Arrhythmias I: Supraventricular Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 34-year-old woman is brought in after a car crash with a heart rate of 128 and a blood pressure of 88/56. The rhythm is regular with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Bedside ultrasound shows free fluid in the abdomen. Which is the most appropriate approach to her heart rate?","choices":["Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Intravenous metoprolol","Treat the bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. This is appropriate sinus tachycardia from hypovolemia, a pathologic cause; management of appropriate sinus tachycardia is treating the underlying cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman has sudden palpitations after three energy drinks. She notices her neck throbbing, and examination shows a frog sign (rapid visible pulsations in the neck). Her heart rate is 172 and regular, with a narrow QRS and no visible P waves. She has no heart disease. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Sinus tachycardia","Atrioventricular nodal reentry"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A paroxysmal, regular narrow tachycardia with hidden P waves, neck pulsations (the frog sign) and a stimulant trigger in a young woman without heart disease is atrioventricular nodal reentrant tachycardia. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman has had 20 minutes of sudden palpitations. Her heart rate is 188, blood pressure 122/76, and she is alert with no chest pain or breathlessness. The electrocardiogram shows a regular narrow complex tachycardia with no visible P waves. Which is the most appropriate first step?","choices":["Intravenous procainamide","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A stable, regular narrow complex tachycardia above 160 with hidden P waves is supraventricular tachycardia, and the first-line treatment when stable is a vagal maneuver, then adenosine. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man had coronary bypass surgery two months ago. He now has palpitations and fatigue. His heart rate is 150 and regular, and the electrocardiogram shows sawtooth waves, two for every narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus tachycardia","Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Sawtooth flutter waves with two for every narrow QRS are atrial flutter with 2:1 block, the most common ratio, and atrial scarring after surgery is a classic setting. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man had cardiac surgery last year. A rhythm strip shows normal sinus beats, then a sudden pause with no P wave, after which narrow beats at 45 appear with no visible P waves. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial flutter","Sinus arrest","Sinus bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In sinus arrest the sinus node does not generate an impulse, leading to a junctional rhythm (narrow beats at 40 to 60 with no visible P waves); injury to the node from cardiac surgery is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 59-year-old man has frequent premature atrial contractions on a monitor (early beats with abnormal P waves), many per minute. He has no known heart disease. Which evaluation is most appropriate?","choices":["No evaluation is needed","Look for structural heart disease","Test for an accessory pathway","Serum digoxin level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If premature atrial contractions are frequent, look for underlying structural heart disease; in a diseased heart they may precede supraventricular tachycardia, fibrillation or flutter. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man's frequent premature atrial contractions are shown on monitoring to trigger his episodes of atrial fibrillation. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Septal myectomy","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Carotid endarterectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation is used if premature atrial contractions are frequent or are triggering atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 42-year-old man has paroxysmal atrial fibrillation and no hypertension, diabetes, heart failure, stroke or vascular disease, so his CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score is 0. Which anticoagulation plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Lifelong direct oral anticoagulant","Heparin infusion at home","May not be needed","Warfarin, target ratio 2 to 3"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score of 0 in a man (or 1 in a woman) is low risk and may not require anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman with Wolff-Parkinson-White has had three emergency visits for reentrant tachycardia this year. Which long-term management is first line?","choices":["Lifelong oral adenosine","Permanent pacemaker","Lifelong anticoagulation","Ablation of the pathway"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation of the accessory pathway is first-line chronic management of Wolff-Parkinson-White, removing the substrate for reentry and pre-excited atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman has had atrial fibrillation for years and asks why her clinician insists on a blood thinner. Which complication is the anticoagulant meant to prevent?","choices":["Stroke from a clot","Heart block","Ventricular fibrillation","A faster heart rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anticoagulation in atrial fibrillation prevents stroke; the CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score is used to assess that risk, which rises with age. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a slow sinus rhythm at 34 is hypotensive and drowsy. Two doses of atropine have not changed his rate or blood pressure. Which is the next step?","choices":["Temporary pacemaker","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion","Oral metoprolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When atropine fails in acute, symptomatic, unstable bradycardia, the next step is a temporary pacemaker; a permanent pacemaker follows if the cause will not resolve. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is told his electrocardiogram shows a normal P wave before each narrow QRS at 68 beats per minute, with the rate rising as he breathes in and falling as he breathes out. He worries that he has heart disease. Which counseling is most accurate?","choices":["He needs an anticoagulant","A normal variant; no treatment","He needs a pacemaker workup","He should stop exercising"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A sinus rate that speeds with inspiration and slows with expiration, with normal P waves and a narrow QRS, is sinus arrhythmia, a normal physiologic variant that needs no treatment. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"An 81-year-old man has known tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) and asks which fast rhythm is behind his palpitations. An ambulatory monitor is arranged. Which rhythm is it most likely to record during the fast episodes?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Junctional rhythm","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In tachy-brady syndrome the tachyarrhythmia that alternates with bradycardia or pauses is most commonly atrial fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with right-sided heart failure has atrial flutter (sawtooth flutter waves) with a ventricular rate of 140. She is alert, with a blood pressure of 124/78 and no chest pain. Which is the most appropriate initial treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Adenosine twice","Permanent pacemaker","A beta or calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically stable atrial flutter is rate-controlled with a beta blocker or calcium channel blocker; rate control is the first goal, though harder to achieve than in fibrillation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man is admitted with an inferior myocardial infarction. His heart rate falls to 46 with narrow QRS complexes and P waves that are either inverted (retrograde P waves) or buried in the QRS. Which rhythm is most likely?","choices":["Junctional rhythm","Sinus bradycardia","Atrial flutter","Focal atrial tachycardia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A regular narrow rhythm at 40 to 60 with inverted or buried P waves is a junctional rhythm, and inferior myocardial infarction is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 75-year-old man has a junctional rhythm at 42 with dizziness. Monitoring shows that the cause is high-grade atrioventricular block. Which treatment is indicated?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Observation","Adenosine","Catheter ablation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A junctional rhythm due to complete or high-grade atrioventricular block or sick sinus syndrome is treated with a permanent pacemaker. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is brought in confused, with a blood pressure of 76/40. The monitor shows a slow, regular rhythm at 36 with a normal P wave before every narrow QRS. Which is the first-line treatment?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Atropine","Adenosine","Synchronized cardioversion"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Acute, symptomatic, hemodynamically unstable sinus bradycardia is treated first with atropine; if it fails, a temporary pacemaker follows, and chronic symptomatic disease gets a permanent one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has new fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance and constipation. Her pulse is 50 and regular, with a normal P wave before each narrow QRS. She takes no medications. Which test is most useful next?","choices":["Thyroid function tests","Serum digoxin level","Electrophysiology study","Ambulatory monitor for pauses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hypothyroidism is a listed cause of sinus bradycardia, and her weight gain, cold intolerance and constipation point to it, so thyroid testing comes next. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman notices occasional skipped beats during a stressful month of heavy coffee and wine. Her electrocardiogram shows occasional early beats with an abnormally shaped P wave and a narrow QRS, and her examination and echocardiogram are normal. Which advice is most appropriate?","choices":["Start a beta blocker","Refer for ablation","Start anticoagulation","Reassure; avoid triggers"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Premature atrial contractions in a normal heart with excess alcohol, stress or caffeine are benign: counsel avoidance of precipitants and reassure about the benign nature of the rhythm. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 76-year-old woman with hypertension and diabetes has nonvalvular atrial fibrillation and a high CHA2DS2-VASc (stroke risk) score. Which anticoagulant class is preferred?","choices":["Aspirin alone","No anticoagulant, given her age","A direct oral anticoagulant","Warfarin as first choice"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Direct oral anticoagulants (dabigatran, apixaban, edoxaban or rivaroxaban) are preferred in nonvalvular atrial fibrillation; a high stroke-risk score means she needs anticoagulation. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has focal atrial tachycardia at 165. He is hemodynamically stable but symptomatic with palpitations. Which acute treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Unsynchronized defibrillation","Intravenous magnesium","Intravenous beta blocker","Intravenous atropine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stable, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia is treated with an intravenous beta blocker or nondihydropyridine calcium channel blocker while the underlying cause is identified and treated. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman has frequent premature junctional contractions, more than 10 per minute, on a monitor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Permanent pacemaker","Anticoagulation","Check for heart disease","Reassurance alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Frequent premature junctional contractions (more than 10 per minute, or multifocal) prompt a search for structural heart disease, with a beta blocker to treat them. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man has tachy-brady syndrome (a fast rhythm alternating with slow rates and pauses) on monitoring, and he faints during the pauses. Thyroid tests and electrolytes are normal and he takes no rate-slowing drugs. Which treatment is most appropriate?","choices":["Atropine as needed","Catheter ablation alone","Observation alone","Permanent pacemaker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When symptoms correlate with bradycardia in sick sinus syndrome a permanent pacemaker is placed, and tachy-brady syndrome often needs one. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with hypertension, diabetes and obstructive sleep apnea has had palpitations for two days. His pulse is irregularly irregular at 132, and the electrocardiogram shows no P waves and a narrow QRS. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Multifocal atrial tachycardia","Atrial flutter with 2:1 block","Sinus arrhythmia","Atrial fibrillation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An irregularly irregular narrow complex rhythm with absent P waves is atrial fibrillation, and hypertension, diabetes, sleep apnea and older age are listed risk factors. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man whose Wolff-Parkinson-White was found on a school electrocardiogram presents with palpitations. The rhythm is irregular and wide-complex, at rates up to 260, with varying delta waves (slurred upstrokes). He is alert with a blood pressure of 118/74. Which drug is the most appropriate treatment?","choices":["Adenosine","Intravenous magnesium","Atropine","Intravenous procainamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Irregular, wide, very fast complexes in a patient with Wolff-Parkinson-White are pre-excited atrial fibrillation, treated with intravenous procainamide; adenosine must never be used. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with atrial fibrillation at 150 has crackles throughout both lungs from pulmonary edema and a blood pressure of 82/50. Which feature makes this presentation a medical emergency?","choices":["Severe heart failure","A heart rate above 100","An irregularly irregular pulse","Her age over 65"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Medical emergencies from atrial fibrillation include severe heart failure (pulmonary edema, hypotension) and atrial fibrillation with an acute myocardial infarction. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has recurrent, symptomatic focal atrial tachycardia despite an oral beta blocker. She wants a definitive option. Referral for which procedure is most appropriate?","choices":["Lifelong anticoagulation","Catheter ablation","Permanent pacemaker","Repeated adenosine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Catheter ablation of the ectopic focus is usually curative for focal atrial tachycardia (about 90%), after oral beta blockers or calcium channel blockers as initial therapy. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"A 54-year-old man has a regular narrow complex tachycardia at 210. His blood pressure is 72/40, and he is confused and sweating. Which is the most appropriate immediate treatment?","choices":["Synchronized cardioversion","Vagal maneuver","Adenosine","Unsynchronized defibrillation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Hemodynamically unstable supraventricular tachycardia (hypotension and confusion) gets immediate synchronized cardioversion, a timed shock at decreased voltage. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"An 82-year-old woman taking digoxin for heart failure has nausea and fatigue. Her heart rate is 48 and regular, with narrow QRS complexes and retrograde P waves (inverted) just after each QRS in II, III and aVF. Which is the most likely cause of this rhythm?","choices":["Digoxin toxicity","Hyperthyroidism","An accessory pathway","Missed digoxin doses"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A slow regular narrow rhythm with inverted P waves after the QRS is a junctional rhythm, and digoxin toxicity is a listed cause. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent atrioventricular nodal reentry has been taught to stop episodes at home with a vagal maneuver. Tonight an episode stopped within minutes after she did so, and she now feels well. What is the most appropriate advice?","choices":["Arrange a pacemaker","No urgent care is usually needed","Come to the emergency department","Start an anticoagulant"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. If a vagal maneuver terminates nodal reentry in a patient who has been educated, urgent medical attention is usually not required. — arrhythmias I - Elwaya-2.pptx, Slide 41"}]};
